<article class="env-variant" id="0568">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0568">Variant <span data-tag="0568">3.5.9.10</span>.</a> Suppose we are given a commutative diagram of Kan complexes </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ X \ar [rr]^{f} \ar [dr] &amp;  &amp;  Y \ar [dl] \\ &amp;  Z, &amp;  } \]
</div>
<p> where the vertical maps are Kan fibrations. Then $f$ is $n$-truncated if and only if, for every vertex $z \in Z$, the induced map $f_{z}: X_{z} \rightarrow Y_{z}$ is $n$-truncated. To prove this, we can use Proposition <a data-tag="00UV" href="/tag/00UV">3.1.8.1</a> to reduce to the case where $f$ is a Kan fibration. In this case, the desired result follows from the criterion of Proposition <a data-tag="0566" href="/tag/0566">3.5.9.8</a> (since a Kan complex can be realized as a fiber of $f$ if and only if it can be realized as a fiber of $f_{z}$ for some vertex $z \in Z$). </p>
</article>
